.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt SED 1
.Os NovaOS
.Sh NAME
.Nm sed
.Nd edit a stream of records
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nErsuz
.Op Fl i Ns Op Ar suffix
.Op Fl e Ar script
.Op Fl f Ar script_file
.Op Fl l Ar width
.Op Fl -sandbox
.Op Fl -follow-symlinks
.Op Fl -posix
.Op Ar script
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Scripts and script files are evaluated in command-line order.
With no input files, the program reads standard input.
By default, each pattern space is printed at the end of its cycle.
Use
.Fl n
for explicit output,
.Fl E
for extended regular expressions and
.Fl z
for NUL-delimited input.
The data locale operates on bytes.
UTF-8 characters may span multiple matches.
.Fl -sandbox
rejects scripts with external effects before they run.
Use
.Fl -help
for other options and
.Fl -version
for the component version.
.Sh FILES
On Darwin,
.Fl i
writes a candidate in the destination directory and optionally keeps a backup.
An unwritable source is refused.
GNU debug traces and full POSIX and multibyte compatibility are unavailable.
On NovaOS P9, in-place replacement is unavailable.
The shell can display this manual and the help page.
File and pipe execution require admission of the ELF by the application catalogue.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
A completed script normally returns 0.
A syntax error returns 1, a missing input file returns 2 and an I/O failure returns 4.
The q and Q commands can return an explicit status.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl sed -n '1,3p' file
.Dl sed -nE 's/^([a-z])([0-9])$/\e2\e1/p' file
